# Reference: Gail Grant, "Technical Manual and Dictionary of Classical Ballet" (Relevé: raised from a plié)
# Reference: Agrippina Vaganova, "Basic Principles of Classical Ballet"
# Review status: Experimental (ballet UAT; pending peer review with dance-domain educator)
# Biomechanical note: Unlike an elevé, a classical relevé begins with a preparatory demi-plié
# before springing or pressing onto demi-pointe (balls of feet), and lowers back with control
# through demi-plié before recovering to straight legs.

posecode exercise "Relevé"
  rig humanoid
  pose start = first-position

  step "Demi-plié" 1.6s flow:
    hips: flex 31.5
    hips: abduct 17
    knees: flex 55
    ankles: dorsiflex 15
    shoulders: abduct 70
    elbows: flex 15
    spine: extend 4
    ground-lock: feet
    cue "Demi-plié in first position, heels flat and knees opening over the turned-out toes"

  step "Rise to relevé" 1.6s settle:
    hips: flex 0
    hips: abduct 0
    knees: extend 0
    ankles: plantarflex 30
    shoulders: abduct 70
    elbows: flex 15
    spine: extend 4
    ground-lock: feet
    cue "Press dynamically from the plié onto the balls of the feet, zipping the legs straight"

  step "Lower through plié" 1.6s flow:
    hips: flex 31.5
    hips: abduct 17
    knees: flex 55
    ankles: dorsiflex 15
    shoulders: abduct 70
    elbows: flex 15
    spine: extend 4
    ground-lock: feet
    cue "Lower the heels smoothly through demi-plié, absorbing the descent without collapsing"

  step "Straighten" 1.4s settle:
    hips: flex 0
    hips: abduct 0
    knees: flex 0
    ankles: dorsiflex 0
    shoulders: abduct 70
    elbows: flex 15
    spine: extend 4
    ground-lock: feet
    cue "Straighten knees to conclude in first position with tall posture and arms maintained"

  repeat 4
